WEBVTT

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:20.750
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. There's this kind of therapy for trauma, victims of sexual assault, soldiers with PTSD, where instead of taking years and talking, and talking, and talking on some couch to a therapist with no end in sight, you basically knock it out, all the treatment in just 10 or 12 sessions.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。针对创伤、性侵受害者和患有创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）的士兵，存在一种治疗方法：与其在治疗师的沙发上耗费数年进行无休止的谈话，不如通过短短10到12次疗程彻底解决问题。

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:39.370
<v Ira Glass>Two weeks-- you can do it in two weeks. And it's effective. Studies have shown that. This therapy's been around since the '80s, but I think lots of people who might find it useful don't even know it's an option for them. Like, for instance, Jaime Lowe, who's a writer and reporter and a good candidate for this treatment.
<v Ira Glass>两周——你两周就能完成。而且疗效显著。研究已经证实了这一点。这种疗法自80年代起就存在了，但我认为许多可能从中受益的人甚至不知道还有这种选择。比如，作家兼记者杰米·洛，她就是这种治疗的理想候选人。

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:54.770
<v Jaime Lowe>I was sexually assaulted when I was 13, almost 30 years ago. And for most of those 30 years, I didn't really talk about it. Not because I didn't want to or need to, but because I didn't know how. And no one asked.
<v Jaime Lowe>我在13岁时遭到性侵，那已经是快30年前的事了。在那30年里，我基本没谈论过这件事。不是因为我不想或不需要，而是因为我不知道怎么说。而且也没有人问过我。

00:00:54.770 --> 00:01:08.480
<v Ira Glass>Jaime Lowe decided to do a story where she would go through this therapy herself and record all of the sessions, which never happens. Like most therapy, usually this treatment is in private, not on the radio.
<v Ira Glass>杰米·洛决定做一个专题报道，亲身尝试这种疗法并记录下所有的疗程，这种情况以前从未有过。像大多数心理治疗一样，这种治疗通常在私下进行，而不是在电台广播。

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:27.560
<v Ira Glass>And hearing how it works, it really is kind of remarkable. My mom was a therapist. I've been in therapy. This is so different from what I have ever heard. And that's going to be our show today. We're going to hear it step by step, this sometimes life-changing process, and how that can happen so quickly.
<v Ira Glass>听到它是如何运作的，确实相当了不起。我母亲曾是心理治疗师，我也接受过治疗。这和我听过的所有疗法都截然不同。这就是我们今天节目的内容。我们将一步步聆听这个有时能改变人生的过程，以及它是如何如此迅速地发生的。

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:37.040
<v Ira Glass>A quick warning to listeners before we start about content. This story does mention the sexual assault of a teenager. Here's Jaime Lowe.
<v Ira Glass>在开始之前，给听众一个简短的内容提醒。本故事提到了青少年时期遭受的性侵。下面是杰米·洛的讲述。

00:01:37.040 --> 00:02:04.010
<v Jaime Lowe>I thought because I didn't talk about the assault or even think about it much, everything was as resolved as it could be. But then after Donald Trump, and "grab them by the pussy," and Harvey Weinstein, and all of them, it's not that specific memories of the assault would pop up, I just felt immobilized, anxious, protective of my body. And I realized my sexual assault wasn't resolved at all.
<v Jaime Lowe>我原以为既然我不谈论那次侵犯，甚至不去多想它，那么一切就算解决得尽可能好了。但后来在唐纳德·特朗普、“抓下体”言论、哈维·温斯坦以及所有人之后，并不是说我对侵犯的具体记忆会突然涌现，我只是感到无法动弹、焦虑、对自己的身体充满保护欲。我意识到我的性侵创伤根本没有解决。

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:27.990
<v Jaime Lowe>Around this time, I heard about a therapy for sexual assault survivors, called Cognitive Processing Therapy, or CPT. A researcher described it to me as short-term inexpensive, practical, like learning a skill. The typical way to deal with trauma in therapy is to talk about the incident over and over, until it's less radioactive.
<v Jaime Lowe>大约在这个时候，我听说了一种针对性侵幸存者的疗法，叫做认知处理疗法（CPT）。一位研究人员向我描述它为短期的、低成本的、实用的，就像学习一项技能。治疗创伤的典型方法是反复谈论事件，直到它不再那么具有杀伤力。

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:57.970
<v Jaime Lowe>CPT does the same thing, but in a systematized way. It's all laid out in advance, and the same for everyone-- what you'll do in the first session, and the second, and the third, and so on. Each session is based on learning a skill and practicing that skill on a worksheet. You go over each element of the trauma, piece by piece, and try to see it differently. The goal is to change the story you've been telling yourself about what happened.
<v Jaime Lowe>CPT做同样的事，但以一种系统化的方式。一切都是提前安排好的，对每个人都一样——你在第一次、第二次、第三次疗程等会做什么。每个疗程都基于学习一项技能并在工作表上练习。你要逐个梳理创伤的每个要素，并尝试从不同的角度看待它。目标是改变你一直以来对自己所发生之事的描述。

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:18.520
<v Jaime Lowe>I got in touch with Dr. Debra Kaysen, a psychologist with a specialty in trauma therapy at the University of Washington School of Medicine. She and I started talking and e-mailing about CPT. It all seemed so different than the therapy I was used to-- my weekly talk sessions-- and I realized I wanted to try it.
<v Jaime Lowe>我联系了华盛顿大学医学院的心理学家、创伤治疗专家黛布拉·凯森博士。我们开始交谈并互通邮件讨论CPT。这与我习惯的每周谈话疗法完全不同，我意识到我想尝试一下。

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:43.700
<v Jaime Lowe>I've done a lot of therapy. After the assault, I had two manic episodes and was diagnosed bipolar. I've always been very open about mental illness. I wrote a book about being bipolar a couple of years ago. But even though that book is partly about my adolescence, I barely mention the assault. I've never dealt with it, I never wanted to-- in the book, in treatment.
<v Jaime Lowe>我做过很多心理治疗。侵犯发生后，我有过两次躁狂发作，并被诊断为躁郁症。我一直对精神疾病持开放态度。几年前我写了一本关于躁郁症的书。但尽管那本书部分关于我的青春期，我几乎没提过那次侵犯。我从未处理过它，也从未想过要处理它——在书里或治疗中。

00:03:43.700 --> 00:04:12.520
<v Jaime Lowe>But now I was feeling the trauma more. I thought CPT might help me. I also thought, what if this could help lots of people? I asked Dr. Kaysen if I could record the sessions and play them on the radio. She checked in with colleagues-- a psychologist with an expertise in journalism, a journalism ethics professor-- to make sure we'd be able to have a therapeutic relationship while I was simultaneously doing a story about the therapy.
<v Jaime Lowe>但现在我感到了更深的创伤。我觉得CPT可能会有所帮助。我也在想，如果这能帮助很多人呢？我问凯森博士我是否可以录下疗程并在广播中播放。她咨询了她的同事——一位擅长新闻学的心理学家和一位新闻伦理学教授——以确保在进行关于该疗法的报道时，我们仍能保持治疗关系。

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:35.110
<v Jaime Lowe>After a lot of back and forth and establishing some basic ground rules, we set up our CPT boot camp-- 10 hour-long sessions over two weeks in Seattle. Usually CPT is one session a week for 12 weeks, but we decided to condense it. And early studies show this approach is effective. Last October, I flew out to Seattle.
<v Jaime Lowe>在反复沟通并制定了一些基本准则后，我们成立了CPT训练营——在西雅图进行为期两周的10次一小时疗程。通常CPT是每周一次，持续12周，但我们决定浓缩它。早期的研究表明这种方法是有效的。去年十月，我飞往西雅图。

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.680
<v Jaime Lowe>Session one, October 22nd.
<v Jaime Lowe>第一次疗程，10月22日。

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:39.320
<v Debra Kaysen>Excellent.
<v Debra Kaysen>太好了。

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.810
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah. OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>嗯。好的。

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:40.960
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.
<v Debra Kaysen>嗯。

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:52.370
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm sitting with Dr. Kaysen in a generic room at the university clinic. I fail at setting up the recording equipment a few times, before getting it semi-right. I'm not a radio person.
<v Jaime Lowe>我和凯森博士坐在大学诊所的一间普通房间里。我在设置录音设备时失败了几次，才勉强弄对。我不是搞广播的人。

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:54.990
<v Debra Kaysen>So let me tell you a little bit about what we're going to do today. OK?
<v Debra Kaysen>那么，让我告诉你我们今天要做什么。好吗？

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:55.340
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:03.880
<v Debra Kaysen>So usually, today is a little more heavy on the psycho ed. It's going to be a little different than a lot of our other sessions. I'm going to be talking a lot.
<v Debra Kaysen>通常，今天的内容会侧重于心理教育。它会和我们其他的疗程有点不同。我会说很多话。

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:04.290
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:07.310
<v Debra Kaysen>OK? Which takes a little bit of the pressure off of you, maybe.
<v Debra Kaysen>好吗？这可能会减轻你的一点压力。

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:08.100
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:35.340
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen has a kindergarten teacher presence that makes me feel ready and OK. She starts the first session with me the way she would with anyone, by talking about sexual assault and PTSD. 50% of women who are sexually assaulted develop PTSD. That could mean symptoms like depression, anxiety, flashbacks-- some of the things I've actually been feeling when I hear the news.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士有一种幼儿园老师般的亲和力，让我感到准备好了，没问题。她像对待任何人一样开始了我们的第一次疗程，谈论性侵和PTSD。50%遭受性侵的女性会患上PTSD。这可能意味着抑郁、焦虑、闪回等症状——这些正是我听到新闻时实际感受到的。

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:58.250
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen hands me a photocopied worksheet. Everything we're going to do, this entire therapy, is structured around these worksheets. I'll do them at my Airbnb. And when Dr. Kaysen and I meet, we'll go over them. Each set of worksheets will cover a new skill, and I'll master that before moving on to the next skill, which Dr. Kaysen will introduce at the end of each session.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士递给我一张复印的工作表。我们要做的每一件事，整个治疗过程，都是围绕这些工作表展开的。我会在爱彼迎（Airbnb）住处完成它们。当我们见面时，会一起回顾。每套工作表涵盖一项新技能，我会在进入下一项技能前掌握它，凯森博士会在每次疗程结束时介绍下一项技能。

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:17.800
<v Jaime Lowe>The idea is, by the time we're done, I'll be able to do this on my own with any issue in my life. Dr. Kaysen explains my first assignment. Most of the worksheets have all sorts of boxes to fill in with answers. They look like badly designed forms you'd get at the DMV, but this first one is just a sheet of paper.
<v Jaime Lowe>想法是，当我们完成时，我将能够独自应对生活中的任何问题。凯森博士解释了我的第一个任务。大多数工作表都有各种各样的格子需要填上答案。它们看起来就像你在车管所拿到的设计拙劣的表格，但这第一张只是一张纸。

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:37.330
<v Debra Kaysen>What I want you to do is write at least one page on why you think that the sexual assault occurred. I do not want you to use this as an opportunity to write specific details about the event, OK? This is more writing about what you think caused the event.
<v Debra Kaysen>我想要你做的是写至少一页关于你认为性侵是如何发生的。我不想让你借此机会写关于事件的具体细节，好吗？这是让你写写你认为导致了该事件的原因。

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:55.520
<v Jaime Lowe>The phrase "caused the event" makes me uneasy, because as much as I intellectually believe that nothing specific really caused the event, there are things I still question. It's embarrassing that, after 30 years, I still might think it's my fault, but I do.
<v Jaime Lowe>“导致了该事件”这句话让我不安，因为尽管从理性上我相信没有具体的什么导致了事件，但我内心仍有质疑的事。令人尴尬的是，30年后，我可能还是认为这是我的错，但我确实这么想。

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.570
<v Jaime Lowe>I can tell you that the cause is going to be very hard for me.
<v Jaime Lowe>我可以告诉你，谈论起因对我来说非常困难。

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:02.190
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah. Yeah, we talked earlier about what are the stories we tell ourselves.
<v Debra Kaysen>是的。是的，我们之前谈到过我们告诉自己的那些故事。

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:03.380
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:04.600
<v Debra Kaysen>It was this.
<v Debra Kaysen>就是这个。

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:05.490
<v Jaime Lowe>Mm-hmm.
<v Jaime Lowe>嗯哼。

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:28.930
<v Debra Kaysen>Right? The event happened because I took a shortcut. Right? The event happened because I had a drink. The event happened because I was wearing a short skirt. And sometimes people know that that's not why it happened, but sometimes those thoughts still haunt them. OK. There aren't any right or wrong answers. I want you to just vomit the ideas on the page.
<v Debra Kaysen>对吧？事件发生是因为我走了捷径。对吧？事件发生是因为我喝了酒。事件发生是因为我穿了短裙。有时人们知道这不是原因，但这些想法仍然会困扰他们。好的。没有对错答案。我只想要你把脑中的想法倒在纸上。

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.020
<v Jaime Lowe>That's my specialty.
<v Jaime Lowe>那是我的专长。

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:30.360
<v Debra Kaysen>Awesome. Then you'll do well.
<v Debra Kaysen>太棒了。那你一定没问题。

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:31.930
<v Jaime Lowe>I love vomiting out words.
<v Jaime Lowe>我喜欢把文字倒出来。

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:34.840
<v Debra Kaysen>That's awesome. That's what I want. All right.
<v Debra Kaysen>太棒了。我想要的就是这个。好了。

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.070
<v Jaime Lowe>OK, so.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的，那么。

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:42.960
<v Debra Kaysen>OK, any worries that you have about doing this?
<v Debra Kaysen>好的，关于做这个你有什么担心吗？

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.380
<v Jaime Lowe>Nope.
<v Jaime Lowe>没有。

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.280
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。

00:07:46.280 --> 00:08:19.160
<v Jaime Lowe>But of course, I'm worried. The thought of reliving the assault is terrifying. Even reading the one-sentence description of the assault during the audio recording of my book left me in tears. I rarely articulated the details out loud. I just don't know what will come up. I don't know what it's going to be like to dig in for the next two weeks.
<v Jaime Lowe>但当然，我很担心。重温那次侵犯的想法太可怕了。即使在录制我的书的有声版时读到对侵犯的那一句描述，都让我泪流满面。我很少大声说出细节。我不知道会发生什么。我不知道在接下来的两周里深入挖掘会是什么感觉。

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.880
<v Jaime Lowe>Session two. So what are you actually doing?
<v Jaime Lowe>第二次疗程。那么你现在在做什么？

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:23.390
<v Debra Kaysen>Right now?
<v Debra Kaysen>现在吗？

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:23.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>对。

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:25.440
<v Debra Kaysen>I'm totaling it.
<v Debra Kaysen>我在统计它。

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:27.290
<v Jaime Lowe>Can you describe the worksheet, actually?
<v Jaime Lowe>你能描述一下这张工作表吗？

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:30.230
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, absolutely. Happy to. So this is called the PTSD checklist.
<v Debra Kaysen>当然。我很乐意。这叫做PTSD检查清单。

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:30.800
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:37.670
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. And all it is a measure of how intense the symptoms of PTSD are.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。它只是衡量PTSD症状有多严重的一个指标。

00:08:37.670 --> 00:09:06.590
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen says we'll start all the sessions like this-- going over my PTSD symptoms and rating their intensity. How frequently have I been thinking of the trauma? Have I been dreaming about it? Reliving it? In the past 24 hours, how much have I been distressed by repeated disturbing and unwanted memories of the traumatic event? Each question gets a numerical value on a scale of 0 to 4, and Dr. Kaysen adds them up.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士说我们将像这样开始所有疗程——回顾我的PTSD症状并评估它们的强度。我多久会想到那次创伤？有没有做关于它的梦？重温它？在过去24小时内，我因反复出现创伤事件的令人不安和不想要的记忆而感到痛苦的程度如何？每个问题都有一个0到4的分值，凯森博士会把它们加起来。

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:12.380
<v Debra Kaysen>And so what I'll do is I'll actually start graphing these and keeping track--
<v Debra Kaysen>我实际上会开始画图表并追踪这些——

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:12.620
<v Jaime Lowe>Cool.
<v Jaime Lowe>酷。

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:13.770
<v Debra Kaysen>--of them.
<v Debra Kaysen>——它们。

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:32.120
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm not sure how to answer these questions with a number, but I have the same problem when physicians ask me to rate pain on a scale of 1 to 10. After we go over my PTSD symptoms, Dr. Kaysen will ask me about my mood. Then we'll move on to the homework, what she calls the practice.
<v Jaime Lowe>我不确定该如何用数字回答这些问题，但当医生让我用1到10的等级评定疼痛时，我也面临同样的问题。回顾完我的PTSD症状后，凯森博士会问我心情如何。然后我们将进入家庭作业，也就是她所谓的“练习”。

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:38.090
<v Debra Kaysen>Most of each session we're going to spend actually reviewing the practice that you did over the day. OK?
<v Debra Kaysen>大部分疗程时间我们将用来回顾你当天完成的练习。好吗？

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:38.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Mm-hmm.
<v Jaime Lowe>嗯哼。

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.550
<v Debra Kaysen>So for today, what we're going to do is we're going to actually start by going through the impact statement that you wrote.
<v Debra Kaysen>所以今天，我们要做的实际上是从回顾你写的“影响陈述”开始。

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:55.680
<v Jaime Lowe>The impact statement-- the one-page worksheet on why I think the assault happened. I wrote it before sunrise this morning, and it was, not surprisingly, really hard to write.
<v Jaime Lowe>影响陈述——那张关于我为什么认为侵犯发生的单页工作表。我今天凌晨写完它的，不出所料，写起来真的很难。

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.280
<v Debra Kaysen>So I'm going to have you read to me what you wrote.
<v Debra Kaysen>那我要你把你写的读给我听。

00:09:58.280 --> 00:09:58.820
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:02.040
<v Debra Kaysen>OK?
<v Debra Kaysen>好吗？

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:29.510
<v Jaime Lowe>Uh, I was young, 13. And I don't think I knew much about actual human nature, friendliness, strangers. It's funny to think that I was not cautious about the very thing kids are always warned of-- strangers. I walked to my bus stop alone every morning. [TEARFUL] It was always early. [WEEPING]
<v Jaime Lowe>呃，那时我很小，13岁。我觉得我不太了解人性、友善、陌生人。想到我竟然对自己总被叮嘱要防范的东西——陌生人——缺乏警惕，真是有趣。我每天早上独自走到巴士站。[泪流满面] 总是很早。[抽泣]

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:32.190
<v Debra Kaysen>You're doing great, Jaime. Just stay with it.
<v Debra Kaysen>你做得很好，杰米。坚持住。

00:10:32.190 --> 00:11:14.570
<v Jaime Lowe>[SOBS] There weren't many people, because it was Los Angeles and no one walks. I used to pass an alley and wave at a man. He was young, maybe late teens, early 20s. Every morning, I'd wave at him. He would walk up the alley. I would cross the alley. Or some mornings, I would walk down the alley against my mom's instructions. But we'd pass, and I'd always wave or smile, which is how I greeted everyone in the neighborhood.
<v Jaime Lowe>[抽泣] 那里没什么人，因为是在洛杉矶，没人步行。我过去常经过一条小巷，向一个人挥手。他很年轻，可能十几岁到二十出头。每天早上，我都会向他挥手。他会走上巷子。我会穿过巷子。或者有时，我不顾我妈的叮嘱走过那条巷子。但我们经过时，我总是挥手或微笑，这是我在街坊邻里打招呼的方式。

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:51.600
<v Jaime Lowe>It didn't seem like he should be any different. And in some ways, in retrospect, I feel like I overcompensated and was extra friendly and maybe invited him in, that my wave somehow signaled to him that this was something I wanted, that my friendliness was misinterpreted as desire. That I should have avoided him, [SOBBING] rather than interact.
<v Jaime Lowe>看起来他不应该有什么不同。在某种程度上，回顾过去，我觉得我有点矫枉过正，太友好了，或许引狼入室，我的挥手不知怎么向他暗示了这是我想要的，我的友善被误解为渴望。我不该与他互动，[抽泣] 而应该避开他。

00:11:51.600 --> 00:12:05.820
<v Jaime Lowe>I break down, but I keep reading. Even though I'd followed Dr. Kaysen's instructions not to write down specific details of the event just yet, they were all coming back to me anyway.
<v Jaime Lowe>我崩溃了，但我继续读下去。尽管我遵循了凯森博士的指示，先不要写下事件的具体细节，但无论如何它们还是都涌上来了。

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:15.680
<v Debra Kaysen>You did an incredible job with that. You did an incredible job.
<v Debra Kaysen>你做得太棒了。你做得太棒了。

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen hands me a Kleenex box.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士递给我一盒纸巾。

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:19.560
<v Jaime Lowe>That's OK, I don't need Kleenex.
<v Jaime Lowe>没关系，我不需要纸巾。

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:20.770
<v Debra Kaysen>Are you sure?
<v Debra Kaysen>你确定吗？

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:23.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah, I'm really into the snot.
<v Jaime Lowe>是啊，我真的很习惯流鼻涕。

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:33.290
<v Debra Kaysen>I was going to say, I find sleeves work fine for me. OK. Is it OK with you if I take a look at it?
<v Debra Kaysen>我本想说，我发现衣袖对我来说就够用了。好的。你介意我看一下吗？

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:36.620
<v Jaime Lowe>Of course.
<v Jaime Lowe>当然。

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:55.340
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. So one of the things was a piece around your mom's instructions. Is that-- and I noticed that those emotions got a little more intense when you got to that.
<v Debra Kaysen>好吧。其中一点关于你母亲的嘱咐。这是——我注意到当你谈到这一点时，那些情绪变得更加强烈了。

00:12:55.340 --> 00:13:07.010
<v Jaime Lowe>That was something that I didn't actually remember until I was writing it this morning. We were never really supposed to walk down the alley, even though it was closer to get to the bus stop.
<v Jaime Lowe>直到今天早上写的时候，我才真正记起这件事。虽然走巷子去巴士站更近，但我们本来是不被允许走那条巷子的。

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:28.190
<v Jaime Lowe>I tell Dr. Kaysen more about my mom's rule and how I broke it. She wonders if this might be a stuck point. Stuck points are the first skill introduced in CPT, and they might be the most important skill of all. In a sense, the entire project of CPT is finding stuck points, and then learning how to unstick them.
<v Jaime Lowe>我告诉凯森博士更多关于我母亲的规定以及我是如何违背它的。她想知道这是否是一个“卡住点”。卡住点是CPT引入的第一项技能，它们可能是所有技能中最重要的一项。从某种意义上说，CPT的整个项目就是寻找卡住点，然后学习如何解开它们。

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:57.560
<v Jaime Lowe>A stuck point is basically something you hold to be true, but might in fact not be true. A stuck point is a belief or a thought that's keeping you stuck in the PTSD, stuck in thinking that it's your fault, or that there's something you could have done to avoid it, or stuck in any number of upsetting or inaccurate or harmful ways of remembering what happened. They're so central to this process that Dr. Kaysen hands me a worksheet titled, Stuck Point Log.
<v Jaime Lowe>卡住点基本上是你认为是真的，但实际上可能并非如此的想法。卡住点是一个让你陷入PTSD的信念或想法，让你陷入认为是自己的错，或者认为你可以做些什么来避免它的想法，或者陷入任何无数令人不安、不准确或有害的记忆方式中。它们在这一过程中至关重要，凯森博士递给我一张标题为“卡住点记录”的工作表。

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:01.610
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. This is going to be a living document that you and I are going to share.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。这将是一个你我共享的动态文档。

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:10.100
<v Jaime Lowe>We'll keep track of all my stuck points here, adding to the list as we identify new ones each session, and crossing them off as I work through them.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们将在这里追踪我所有的卡住点，在每次疗程中识别出新的卡住点就添加到列表中，并在我克服它们时把它们划掉。

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:23.570
<v Debra Kaysen>So I may be hearing a little bit of a stuck point, maybe, around like, I should've listened to my mom, or if I hadn't gone that way, it wouldn't have happened.
<v Debra Kaysen>所以我可能听到了一点卡住点的端倪，也许是关于“我不应该那样做”或者“如果我不走那条路就不会发生了”。

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:26.740
<v Jaime Lowe>I think it's more like if I hadn't waved, it wouldn't have happened.
<v Jaime Lowe>我觉得更像是如果我没挥手，就不会发生了。

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:27.500
<v Debra Kaysen>Perfect.
<v Debra Kaysen>完美。

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.740
<v Jaime Lowe>That actually feels more like--
<v Jaime Lowe>这实际上感觉更像——

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:30.730
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.
<v Debra Kaysen>是的。

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.260
<v Jaime Lowe>--what, um.
<v Jaime Lowe>——什么，嗯。

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:36.570
<v Debra Kaysen>So write that down. We're going to start keeping track of these as we find them.
<v Debra Kaysen>把它写下来。我们一发现它们就要开始追踪。

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:36.960
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:42.920
<v Debra Kaysen>So if I hadn't waved, it wouldn't have happened. OK. All right.
<v Debra Kaysen>如果我没挥手，就不会发生了。好的。好。

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:46.400
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen asked me what feelings are coming up.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士问我产生了什么感觉。

00:14:46.400 --> 00:15:06.150
<v Jaime Lowe>I mean, I hear the word shame associated a lot with things like this. And I feel like that would apply, but it doesn't feel like the right word. Like, it feels more just like I want to just shelter myself, and I want to just cocoon.
<v Jaime Lowe>我的意思是，我听到羞耻感这个词常与这类事情联系在一起。我觉得这适用，但这感觉不是准确的词。就像，感觉更像是我想躲避自己，想躲进蚕茧里。

00:15:06.150 --> 00:15:08.620
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:18.230
<v Jaime Lowe>I think that I also just don't like that word. Like, I think that there's something shameful about shame. It's like-- I don't know.
<v Jaime Lowe>我想我也不喜欢那个词。我觉得羞耻感本身是有某种令人羞愧的东西。就像——我不知道。

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:23.220
<v Debra Kaysen>So I think that's going to be an important one for us to sort out, actually, because feelings aren't good or bad.
<v Debra Kaysen>所以我认为这将是我们整理的一个重要方面，实际上，因为情绪没有好坏之分。

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:23.940
<v Jaime Lowe>Right.
<v Jaime Lowe>对。

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:25.220
<v Debra Kaysen>Right?
<v Debra Kaysen>对吧？

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:27.640
<v Jaime Lowe>Isn't there another word, though?
<v Jaime Lowe>不过，没有别的词了吗？

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:44.450
<v Jaime Lowe>I bristle at the word "shame." I always have. There's no room for shame. It sounds weak to me. Like, if you have shame, you have something to feel shameful about. With shame comes softness, and vulnerability, and fragility.
<v Jaime Lowe>我对“羞耻”这个词感到反感。一直以来都是。没有羞耻的余地。它对我来说听起来很软弱。就像，如果你有羞耻感，你就有了值得感到羞愧的事情。羞耻伴随着柔软、脆弱和虚弱。

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:50.360
<v Debra Kaysen>So you will see these lovely worksheets. These are called ABC sheets. OK?
<v Debra Kaysen>所以你会看到这些可爱的工作表。这些叫ABC表格。好的？

00:15:50.360 --> 00:16:27.500
<v Jaime Lowe>It's the end of the session, and ABC is the new skill. I'm supposed to fill in the A column with the activating event-- in my case, the sexual assault-- B with my belief or stuck point about the event, C with the consequence of that belief or stuck point. It's kind of clunky, but the idea seems to be to get you to see that your stuck points are not rational and that hanging on to these irrational stuck points has real emotional consequences, that continuing to tell yourself this stuff as fact makes you feel bad.
<v Jaime Lowe>疗程结束了，ABC是新技能。我应该在A栏填入激活事件——就我而言，是那次性侵——B填入关于该事件的信念或卡住点，C填入该信念或卡住点的后果。这有点笨拙，但想法似乎是让你看到你的卡住点不是理性的，坚持这些非理性的卡住点会产生真实的情感后果，即不断地告诉自己这些事实会让你感觉糟糕。

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:31.890
<v Debra Kaysen>Last thoughts, questions? How did this work for you today?
<v Debra Kaysen>最后有什么想法、问题吗？你今天觉得怎么样？

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:44.960
<v Jaime Lowe>Um, good. It was intense. I mean, I was like-- it was definitely-- I feel weirdly better, because I feel like we've started.
<v Jaime Lowe>嗯，好。很强烈。我的意思是，我感觉——绝对是——我感到奇怪地好一些了，因为我觉得我们开始了。

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:51.750
<v Debra Kaysen>We've started. All right, I'm going to make a copy of the impact statement. So I've got that, all right?
<v Debra Kaysen>我们开始了。好的，我要复印一下影响陈述。我有那个了，好吗？

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:55.330
<v Jaime Lowe>I think session two's done.
<v Jaime Lowe>我想第二次疗程结束了。

00:16:55.330 --> 00:17:06.530
<v Debra Kaysen>I think we're done.
<v Debra Kaysen>我想我们完成了。

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:11.840
<v Jaime Lowe>Session three-- so this is hard for me to answer. Is the unwanted memory ...
<v Jaime Lowe>第三次疗程——这个问题对我来说很难回答。是不想要的记忆……

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:21.860
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm sitting with Dr. Kaysen going over the checklist of my PTSD symptoms. Did I have memories about the assault? Was I dreaming about it? Reliving it?
<v Jaime Lowe>我坐着和凯森博士回顾我的PTSD症状清单。我有关于那次侵犯的记忆吗？我有做关于它的梦吗？重温它吗？

00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:27.260
<v Jaime Lowe>I do feel like I was reliving a little.
<v Jaime Lowe>我确实感觉我重温了一点。

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:51.320
<v Jaime Lowe>We finish the checklist and take out the worksheets. I'd done seven the night before. Because I'm doing a crash course in CPT, each session each day covers a week's worth of treatment. It feels like I'm cramming, but Dr. Kaysen tells me she has patients who are still doing worksheets in the waiting room before sessions. That makes me feel a little better.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们完成了清单并拿出了工作表。我前一天晚上做了七个。因为我在进行CPT的速成课程，每天的每个疗程都涵盖了一周的治疗量。感觉像在死记硬背，但凯森博士告诉我有些病人甚至在疗程前的候诊室里还在做工作表。这让我感觉好了一些。

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:58.640
<v Jaime Lowe>We pick up with the stuck point we started on yesterday. If I hadn't said hi, he wouldn't have assaulted me.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们接着昨天开始的卡住点：“如果我没打招呼，他就不会侵犯我。”

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:01.820
<v Debra Kaysen>Had you interacted with this guy beforehand?
<v Debra Kaysen>你之前和这个人有过互动吗？

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:05.980
<v Jaime Lowe>It had been several weeks, probably, of saying hi.
<v Jaime Lowe>可能已经有几周了，都在打招呼。

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:11.150
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. And did anything happen on those other days where you said hi?
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。在那几天你打招呼时发生过什么吗？

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:12.020
<v Jaime Lowe>No.
<v Jaime Lowe>没有。

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:20.330
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen has clearly heard similar stuck points. She pokes holes in my logic until it's obvious that there's another way to see it.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士显然听过类似的卡住点。她向我的逻辑戳洞，直到显而易见有另一种看待方式。

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:23.630
<v Debra Kaysen>Could he have assaulted you if you had never said hi?
<v Debra Kaysen>如果你从没打过招呼，他可能侵犯你吗？

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:29.400
<v Jaime Lowe>Yes. The likelihood that that was the cause is very small to none.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。那种情况是起因的可能性很小甚至为零。

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:31.100
<v Debra Kaysen>You were a little girl.
<v Debra Kaysen>你当时还是个小女孩。

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:32.390
<v Jaime Lowe>Right.
<v Jaime Lowe>对。

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:34.630
<v Debra Kaysen>Right? You were a little girl going to school.
<v Debra Kaysen>对吧？你是个去上学的小女孩。

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:52.130
<v Jaime Lowe>Right. But still, I wouldn't have said I was a little girl. Like, I felt like I was someone who was able to operate in the world and have conversations and interactions with adults, and have responsibility.
<v Jaime Lowe>对。但我还是不会说我是个小女孩。就像，我感觉自己是一个能够在世界上运作、与成年人进行对话和互动、并有责任感的人。

00:18:52.130 --> 00:19:08.930
<v Jaime Lowe>I associate the words "little girl" with a kind of unformed, helpless pink thing covered in ruffles. I didn't relate. At 13, I was babysitting. I had crushes and fantasies. I happily went to the movies or thrifting by myself.
<v Jaime Lowe>我把“小女孩”这个词与某种未成形的、无助的、被褶边覆盖的粉色物体联系起来。我没关联上。13岁时，我做过保姆。我有过暗恋和幻想。我很高兴独自去看电影或去旧货店。

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:34.560
<v Jaime Lowe>We move on to another one of the stuck points, which was covering something I hadn't thought about for a long time-- what I was wearing on the day of the assault. I wore floral boxers from The Gap and a matching solid colored T-shirt. While doing the worksheets this morning, I realized that some part of me still thought the assault was my fault for wearing men's boxers as shorts. And I tell Dr. Kaysen about this.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们转向另一个卡住点，它涉及到我很久没想过的事情——侵犯那天我穿了什么。我穿了盖普（The Gap）的花纹平角裤和配套的纯色T恤。今天早上做工作表时，我意识到自己内心某处仍然认为因为我穿男式平角裤当短裤，所以那次侵犯是我的错。我告诉了凯森博士。

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:40.760
<v Jaime Lowe>I mean, I liked thinking about what I was wearing and trying to wear cute things.
<v Jaime Lowe>我是说，我喜欢思考我穿什么，尝试穿可爱的衣服。

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:47.360
<v Jaime Lowe>She assumes that there was some kind of sexiness attached to the outfit for me, but that wasn't it.
<v Jaime Lowe>她认为这套服装对我来说带有一种性感色彩，但事实并非如此。

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.280
<v Jaime Lowe>Or I didn't wear a sexy outfit.
<v Jaime Lowe>或者说我没穿性感的衣服。

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:50.580
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. OK.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。好的。

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:59.690
<v Jaime Lowe>Like, I have never really worn makeup or been good at that kind of thing. It's just like not my-- like, I don't--
<v Jaime Lowe>比如，我从来没真正化过妆，也不擅长那种东西。这只是不是我的——就像，我不——

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:00.320
<v Debra Kaysen>Not your thing.
<v Debra Kaysen>不是你的风格。

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:00.660
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:01.310
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:14.610
<v Jaime Lowe>The outfit wasn't sexy. These were baggy clothes that masked my body. But even as the assault was happening, I remember standing there and thinking it was somehow because of the boxers.
<v Jaime Lowe>那套衣服不性感。那是遮盖我身体的宽松衣服。但即使在侵犯发生时，我记得站在那里，认为不知怎么就是因为那条平角裤。

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:37.460
<v Jaime Lowe>I remember being very, like, associating the exact outfit exactly with what was happening. I remember that it was because it was an elastic waistband. That seemed like it was, like, there was more access to me, or that like there was something about the article of clothing and the choice of it that felt--
<v Jaime Lowe>我记得很清楚，就像把那套衣服与正在发生的事情直接关联起来。我记得那是因为那是弹性腰带。那看起来像是，因为那意味着有更多接触我的途径，或者说是某种关于那件衣服和选择它的理由感觉——

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:42.700
<v Debra Kaysen>Like that maybe in some way caused it, even if it wasn't the sexy factor.
<v Debra Kaysen>就像在某种程度上导致了它，即使不是因为性感因素。

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:43.510
<v Jaime Lowe>Right.
<v Jaime Lowe>对。

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:49.820
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. So it might not be the sexy factor, but it might be an access factor.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。所以它可能不是性感因素，但可能是可接触性因素。

00:20:49.820 --> 00:20:53.510
<v Jaime Lowe>Or like inappropriate. Like maybe inappropriate clothing.
<v Jaime Lowe>或者就像是不恰当。就像可能穿着不恰当。

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:54.950
<v Debra Kaysen>Interesting. OK. All right.
<v Debra Kaysen>有趣。好的。好吧。

00:20:54.950 --> 00:21:16.610
<v Jaime Lowe>I remember picking out the outfit at The Gap. And I remember putting it in the giveaway pile months later and being asked why I was giving it away since it looked brand new. By the end of the worksheet, my thinking shifts. I articulate my new thought about the boxers-- a more balanced thought, Dr. Kaysen calls it.
<v Jaime Lowe>我记得在盖普挑选那套衣服。我记得几个月后把它放进捐赠堆，并被问到为什么既然看起来像全新的还要捐掉。工作表结束时，我的想法改变了。我表达了我对平角裤的新看法——凯森博士称之为更平衡的想法。

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:29.130
<v Debra Kaysen>You know, you came up with this really lovely balanced thought, which is it doesn't matter what you wear. How much do you believe that?
<v Debra Kaysen>你知道，你想出了这个非常可爱的平衡想法，那就是穿什么并不重要。你相信这个吗？

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:36.420
<v Jaime Lowe>I believe that. I think it can happen to anyone. I mean, I think it's not-- it has nothing to do with what you're wearing.
<v Jaime Lowe>我相信。我想这可能发生在任何人身上。我的意思是，我认为这不是——它与你穿什么无关。

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:37.320
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.
<v Debra Kaysen>是的。

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:54.670
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm starting to understand Dr. Kaysen's technique with these stuck points. She asks questions so I can arrive at answers myself. My previous belief shifts because I've concluded that it's inaccurate. She's my guide, but I have to come to the conclusion myself.
<v Jaime Lowe>我开始理解凯森博士运用这些卡住点的技巧了。她提问，这样我就可以自己得出答案。我之前的信念转变了，因为我得出结论它是不准确的。她是我的向导，但我必须自己得出结论。

00:21:54.670 --> 00:22:04.690
