<v Debra Kaysen>OK? So how this event may have affected your ideas about safety.
<v Debra Kaysen>好吗？所以这个事件是如何影响你关于安全的想法的。

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:20.660
<v Jaime Lowe>Mm-hmm.
<v Jaime Lowe>嗯哼。

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:37.490
<v Jaime Lowe>We practice a couple stuck points on the big mama worksheet. One of them is about how I'm feeling uncomfortable in Seattle. I mention that the Dodgers are playing in the World Series, and I'm hesitant to walk the few blocks to the bar at the end of my street. But I really want to watch the game.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们在“大妈”工作表上练习了几个卡住点。其中一个关于我在西雅图感到不舒服。我提到道奇队正在打世界大赛，我犹豫要不要走几条街到我街尾的酒吧去。但我真的很想看比赛。

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:50.060
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen says that I should consider trying to go to the bar to watch the game, that it's actually not risky behavior. She's not a big baseball fan, so I almost feel like the assignment is for both of us.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士说我应该考虑去酒吧看比赛，其实那不是冒险行为。她不是棒球迷，所以我几乎觉得这个任务是给我们俩布置的。

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:52.670
<v Debra Kaysen>You know, I'm going to ask you on Monday whether you saw the game.
<v Debra Kaysen>你知道，周一我会问你有没有看比赛。

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:53.580
<v Jaime Lowe>Went to it. [LAUGHS]
<v Jaime Lowe>去看了。[笑]

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:57.750
<v Debra Kaysen>Yes. Yes. OK. All right. So in terms of--
<v Debra Kaysen>是的。是的。好的。所以关于——

00:33:57.750 --> 00:33:59.960
<v Jaime Lowe>And I'll ask you if you know what the score was.
<v Jaime Lowe>而且我会问你比分是多少。

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:04.580
<v Debra Kaysen>Oh. Oh. [LAUGHS] Hmm.
<v Debra Kaysen>哦。哦。[笑] 嗯。

00:34:04.580 --> 00:34:04.960
<v Jaime Lowe>There are several games, actually.
<v Jaime Lowe>其实有好几场比赛。

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:10.670
<v Debra Kaysen>But I will have to actually-- I hate to tell you this, but I'm really treatment resistant.
<v Debra Kaysen>但我不得不实际上——我讨厌告诉你这一点，但我真的很“治疗抗拒”。

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:14.650
<v Jaime Lowe>[LAUGHS] This sounds like a stuck point.
<v Jaime Lowe>[笑] 这听起来像是一个卡住点。

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:15.790
<v Debra Kaysen>It could be a stuck point.
<v Debra Kaysen>这可能是一个卡住点。

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:17.050
<v Jaime Lowe>We could get through the baseball stuck point.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们可以克服棒球卡住点。

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:19.460
<v Debra Kaysen>[LAUGHS]
<v Debra Kaysen>[笑]

00:34:19.460 --> 00:34:20.660
<v Jaime Lowe>We can work together on this.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们可以一起解决这个问题。

00:34:20.660 --> 00:34:23.090
<v Debra Kaysen>Absolutely. We'll set it as a long-term treatment goal.
<v Debra Kaysen>当然。我们将把它设为长期治疗目标。

00:34:23.090 --> 00:34:23.660
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:34:23.660 --> 00:34:26.270
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. So in terms of resolutions--
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。所以关于决议——

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:38.450
<v Jaime Lowe>As hard as this week has been, it helps that I trust Dr. Kaysen. She's a good guide.
<v Jaime Lowe>尽管这一周很难，但信任凯森博士对我有所帮助。她是位好向导。

00:34:38.450 --> 00:34:55.800
<v Ira Glass>Jaime Lowe. Coming up, if you learn something important about yourself from a worksheet, is it OK to feel resentful that you learned it from a worksheet? That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>杰米·洛。接下来，如果你从工作表中了解到了关于自己的重要信息，感到愤愤不平是因为你是从工作表中了解到的，这可以吗？一会儿回来，芝加哥公共广播，我们的节目继续。

00:34:55.800 --> 00:35:22.690
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, Ten Sessions. What can you do with trauma from long ago that's never healed? If you're just tuning in, writer Jaime Lowe heard about a kind of therapy called CPT, Cognitive Processing Therapy, that helps people deal with unhealed trauma from sexual assault or combat PTSD, incredibly, in just 10 or 12 sessions. And she decided to try it herself. We pick up halfway through the therapy.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目是“十次疗程”。对于从未愈合的很久以前的创伤，你能做什么？如果你刚调频收听，作家杰米·洛听说了一种叫CPT（认知处理疗法）的疗法，能帮助人们解决性侵或战斗PTSD带来的未愈合创伤，令人难以置信的是，只需10或12次疗程。她决定亲自尝试。我们在治疗进行到一半时继续。

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:24.340
<v Jaime Lowe>Session six.
<v Jaime Lowe>第六次疗程。

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:30.910
<v Debra Kaysen>All right, so in general, since I last saw you on Friday, how has your mood been?
<v Debra Kaysen>好的，那么总体来说，自周五上次见你以来，心情如何？

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:55.200
<v Jaime Lowe>It's Monday, and I've self-cared my way through the weekend, which is another way to say that I went to a handful of vintage clothing stores. I bought a poncho the colors of Mardi Gras. I did not buy the T-shirt that said "Rain, and Coffee, and Salmon, and Weed." The Dodgers lost the series, but I went to the bar to watch the game, and it was fine. It was actually nice.
<v Jaime Lowe>今天是周一，我通过周末的“自我关怀”撑过来了，换句话说，我逛了几家古着店。我买了一件马尔迪格拉斯色彩的披风。我没有买那件印着“雨、咖啡、鲑鱼和大麻”的T恤。道奇队输掉了系列赛，但我还是去酒吧看了比赛，情况还可以。其实还挺不错的。

00:35:55.200 --> 00:36:00.350
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. So do you want to check levels? And then we will delve in.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。你想检查音频电平吗？然后我们深入探讨。

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:04.580
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah. Why don't you speak a tiny bit?
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。你为什么不稍微说几句？

00:36:04.580 --> 00:36:19.780
<v Ira Glass>We check the audio levels. I still have no idea if I'm doing it right. I tell myself, worst case scenario, I won't be able to share the most traumatic moments of my life with millions of people. And that seems like an OK outcome.
<v Ira Glass>我们检查音频电平。我仍然不知道我做得对不对。我告诉自己，最坏的情况是，我没法与数百万人分享我生命中最具创伤性的时刻。那似乎是一个可以接受的结果。

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:32.250
<v Ira Glass>I take out the big mama worksheets I did over the weekend. Each sheet took a lot longer than I expected. Dr. Kaysen picks out a worksheet with the stuck point, I can't protect myself.
<v Ira Glass>我拿出周末做的“大妈”工作表。每一张表花费的时间都比我预期的要长。凯森博士挑选了一张写有“我无法保护自己”这个卡住点的工作表。

00:36:32.250 --> 00:36:38.310
<v Debra Kaysen>OK, I can't protect myself. Oh, good. This is a great one to do, also, with you going out to the bar, too.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的，“我无法保护自己”。哦，好。这也是一个很好的练习，尤其是在你去酒吧之后。

00:36:38.310 --> 00:36:38.480
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:39.930
<v Debra Kaysen>Like really relevant.
<v Debra Kaysen>真的很相关。

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:50.410
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen pauses. It seems like she's seeing a stuck point in the stuck point, which happens a lot-- Russian dolls of stuck points.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士停顿了一下。看起来她在卡住点中又看到了一个卡住点，这种情况经常发生——像俄罗斯套娃一样的卡住点。

00:36:50.410 --> 00:36:56.280
<v Debra Kaysen>Let me ask you a question also. There's another piece here. Are you different now than you were then?
<v Debra Kaysen>让我问你一个问题。这儿还有一部分。你现在和当时不同了吗？

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:56.830
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:36:56.830 --> 00:36:58.240
<v Debra Kaysen>How so?
<v Debra Kaysen>怎么不同？

00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:04.850
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm older. I know more. I've learned from that experience.
<v Jaime Lowe>我长大了。我知道得更多。我从那个经历中学到了东西。

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:07.780
<v Debra Kaysen>Mm-hmm.
<v Debra Kaysen>嗯哼。

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:11.850
<v Jaime Lowe>This reminds me of that song Into the Woods, the musical.
<v Jaime Lowe>这让我想起了那首音乐剧《走进森林》里的歌。

00:37:11.850 --> 00:37:12.550
<v Debra Kaysen>Ah.
<v Debra Kaysen>啊。

00:37:12.550 --> 00:37:17.120
<v Jaime Lowe>The Little Red Riding Hood song, which is basically all about sexual trauma.
<v Jaime Lowe>小红帽那首歌，基本上全是关于性创伤的。

00:37:17.120 --> 00:38:00.230
<v Ira Glass>There's a song from Stephen Sondheim's musical, Into the Woods, that I used to listen to over and over after the attack. The wolf beckons Little Red Riding Hood. She strays from the path, and then she sings, "He drew me close and he swallowed me down, down a dark slimy path where life's secrets that I never want to know and when everything familiar seemed to disappear forever." I mean, those lyrics described how I felt in the aftermath of the assault to a tee, that everything familiar seemed to disappear forever.
<v Ira Glass>史蒂芬·桑德海姆的音乐剧《走进森林》里有一首歌，那次袭击后我曾反复听。大狼引诱小红帽。她偏离了路径，然后唱道：“他把我拉近，把我吞了下去，沿着一条黑暗、黏糊糊的路径，那里有我永远不想知道的生活秘密，当所有熟悉的事物似乎永远消失了。”我的意思是，这些歌词精准地描述了那次侵犯后我的感受，所有熟悉的一切似乎都永远消失了。

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:29.210
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Kaysen and I walk through the worksheet, step by step, through all the prompts and questions CPT relies on-- whether there's evidence for my stuck point, whether I'm making an all or nothing statement, whether I'm jumping to conclusions, what I'm leaving out. I realize that I still feel angry with myself for freezing, that I didn't scream sooner, hit him, run, or defend myself. Dr. Kaysen reminds me that he had a knife.
<v Ira Glass>凯森博士和我一步步地走过工作表，通过CPT所依赖的所有提示和问题——是否存在支持我卡住点的证据，我是否在做非黑即白的陈述，我是否在匆忙下结论，我遗漏了什么。我意识到我仍然对自己因为僵住而感到愤怒，没能早点尖叫、打他、逃跑或自我防御。凯森博士提醒我他有刀。

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:36.980
<v Debra Kaysen>If somebody's got a weapon, sometimes freezing is the best response in that moment.
<v Debra Kaysen>如果有人有武器，有时僵住是那一刻最好的反应。

00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:43.840
<v Jaime Lowe>Or the only response. We continue going through it. And eventually, I reach a more balanced thought.
<v Jaime Lowe>或者是唯一的反应。我们继续进行下去。最终，我得出了一个更平衡的想法。

00:38:43.840 --> 00:38:50.090
<v Debra Kaysen>Good. I did what I could to protect myself from physical harm. Absolutely. That's beautiful that you came up with that.
<v Debra Kaysen>好。我尽我所能保护自己免受身体伤害。绝对。你能得出这个结论太棒了。

00:38:50.090 --> 00:39:08.210
<v Jaime Lowe>The big mama worksheets ask for percentages related to how much you feel things and to how much you believe certain thoughts. At the beginning of this worksheet, I believed I can't protect myself 70%. By the end, I'm in a different place.
<v Jaime Lowe>“大妈”工作表要求填入与你的感受强度和对某些想法的信任度相关的百分比。在这张工作表开始时，我以70%的程度相信“我无法保护自己”。到最后，我处于一个不同的位置。

00:39:08.210 --> 00:39:13.790
<v Debra Kaysen>And so you've managed to get that from 70% to 20%. That's some really nice movement around that.
<v Debra Kaysen>所以你成功地将它从70%降到了20%。围绕这一点有非常好的进展。

00:39:13.790 --> 00:39:21.990
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah. I don't fully understand the percentages, but I did feel better after working through it.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。我不完全理解这些百分比，但在处理完之后我确实感觉好多了。

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:37.460
<v Debra Kaysen>Nice. And fear came down, anger came down, and frustration came down. Nice. That's lovely. And you've also got some other balanced thoughts in here, too, like, I can protect myself most of the time.
<v Debra Kaysen>好。恐惧降下来了，愤怒降下来了，挫败感也降下来了。好。太棒了。你在这里还有其他一些平衡的想法，比如，“我大部分时间都能保护自己。”

00:39:37.460 --> 00:39:37.760
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:46.580
<v Debra Kaysen>You can think about how does that feel, versus I can't protect myself at all. Right. Beautiful job with that.
<v Debra Kaysen>你可以考虑一下那是什么感觉，对比“我根本无法保护自己”。对。做得漂亮。

00:39:46.580 --> 00:40:04.880
<v Jaime Lowe>Suddenly, I'm getting the worksheets. They're still difficult, because thinking is difficult. But that night, I find that doing them is calming and clear. The next day, in session seven, I tell Dr. Kaysen that there's something about the writing that's really key.
<v Jaime Lowe>突然间，我理解这些工作表了。它们依然困难，因为思考是困难的。但那天晚上，我发现做这些练习让我感到冷静且清晰。第二天，在第七次疗程中，我告诉凯森博士，关于写作的某件事真的很关键。

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:13.780
<v Jaime Lowe>And so when I'm writing it down, I'm just seeing that even just subtle shifts are really different when you write it.
<v Jaime Lowe>所以当我写下它时，我发现即使只是微妙的转变，当你写下来时也真的不同。

00:40:13.780 --> 00:40:14.590
<v Debra Kaysen>That's right.
<v Debra Kaysen>没错。

00:40:14.590 --> 00:40:26.320
<v Jaime Lowe>I can see the beginning point. And then I see the end, and I'm just like, ah. It's like you're forehead-slapping. You're just like, oh, yeah, that starting point was really off, like, that's not a rational way to think.
<v Jaime Lowe>我可以看到起始点。然后我看到了终点，我只是想，啊。这就像你额头被猛击一下。你只是想，噢，对了，那个起点真的很偏离，就像，那不是一种理性的思考方式。

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:35.500
<v Debra Kaysen>Right. But I love what I'm hearing you say is, also, you're really seeing some cognitive shifts with doing these. So it's hard work, but you're also seeing some payout.
<v Debra Kaysen>对。但我喜欢听到你所说的，你确实通过这些练习看到了认知的转变。这很辛苦，但你也看到了回报。

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:39.940
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah. I think I'm also a little resentful that it's just coming from a worksheet.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。我也对这仅仅源于一张工作表感到有点愤愤不平。

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:40.280
<v Debra Kaysen>[LAUGHS]
<v Debra Kaysen>[笑]

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:42.250
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm serious. Like, there's something like--
<v Jaime Lowe>我是认真的。就像，有什么——

00:40:42.250 --> 00:40:42.820
<v Debra Kaysen>Why?
<v Debra Kaysen>为什么？

00:40:42.820 --> 00:41:08.290
<v Jaime Lowe>Because it's like-- I can't describe it, but it's sort of like-- OK, I think I can describe it. I think that in talk therapy or traditional analysis, it's so individualized, it's so cocooned, it's so specified to your relationship with one person in that space and that time. And it's very much like a special thing.
<v Jaime Lowe>因为就像——我说不上来，但它有点像——好的，我想我能描述它。我觉得在谈话疗法或传统的分析中，它非常个性化，非常像茧一样，非常特定于你在那个空间和那个时间与一个人的关系。这非常像一件特别的事情。

00:41:08.290 --> 00:41:17.520
<v Jaime Lowe>Whereas a worksheet, it's an equation. And so it's more generic, which actually makes it way better in a lot of ways.
<v Jaime Lowe>然而工作表，它是一个公式。所以它在许多方面其实更通用，这使它在很多方面变得更好。

00:41:17.520 --> 00:41:19.590
<v Debra Kaysen>But also hard because it feels less special.
<v Debra Kaysen>但也很难，因为它感觉没那么特别。

00:41:19.590 --> 00:41:27.870
<v Jaime Lowe>Right, but also kind of like not this precious thing. You're special, and your problems are special.
<v Jaime Lowe>对，但也确实不像是那种珍贵的东西。你是特别的，你的问题也是特别的。

00:41:27.870 --> 00:41:29.650
<v Debra Kaysen>Huh, that's interesting.
<v Debra Kaysen>嗯，真有趣。

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:45.280
<v Jaime Lowe>I like that the goal of CPT is to have the tools to be your own therapist. Several CPT experts told me their role as clinicians was to put themselves out of business. I'm not sure that's true of talk therapy-- for me, anyway.
<v Jaime Lowe>我喜欢CPT的目标是拥有成为自己心理治疗师的工具。几位CPT专家告诉我，他们作为临床医生的角色就是让自己“失业”。我不确定谈话疗法也是这样——至少对我来说不是。

00:41:45.280 --> 00:42:10.060
<v Jaime Lowe>I don't set an end goal. With CPT, I have. I know exactly what I want to work on, and I'm drilling down on that one thing. I'm dealing with the after effects of sexual assault. And I have a list of stuck points to show for it-- 31 different sentences written sloppily on a piece of paper, like a list of groceries. Dr. Kaysen asks me if I've crossed any off.
<v Jaime Lowe>我没有设定结束目标。而通过CPT，我有了。我知道我确切地想解决什么，我正在钻研那一件事。我正在处理性侵后的余波。我有一份卡住点列表作为证明——31个潦草写在纸上的不同句子，就像一张杂货清单。凯森博士问我是否划掉了几个。

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:18.190
<v Jaime Lowe>I crossed off a bunch that I just didn't think were stuck points in the first place. And then I crossed off some that I felt like we worked through.
<v Jaime Lowe>我划掉了一堆我觉得根本不是卡住点的内容。然后我划掉了一些我觉得我们已经解决的。

00:42:18.190 --> 00:42:19.420
<v Debra Kaysen>That's fantastic.
<v Debra Kaysen>太棒了。

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:20.290
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:42:20.290 --> 00:42:41.770
<v Ira Glass>If I hadn't said hi, he wouldn't have assaulted me-- done with that. I can't trust my judgment-- crossed off. Because it wasn't worse, I should be functioning better-- gone. I've been working so hard to learn the process of CPT that I didn't realize how much I'd resolved along the way.
<v Ira Glass>“如果我没打招呼，他就不会侵犯我”——解决了。“我无法相信自己的判断”——划掉了。“因为没更糟，我应该表现得更好”——没了。我一直努力在学习CPT的过程，没意识到我在这一路上解决了这么多。

00:42:41.770 --> 00:42:57.800
<v Ira Glass>I leave the session feeling a sense of accomplishment. I love making lists. I love crossing things off my lists. This is starting to feel satisfying.
<v Ira Glass>我带着成就感离开了疗程。我喜欢列清单。我喜欢把东西从清单上划掉。这开始让我感到满足了。

00:42:57.800 --> 00:42:59.120
<v Jaime Lowe>You check my levels, and I'll check yours--
<v Jaime Lowe>你检查我的电平，我检查你的——

00:42:59.120 --> 00:43:00.310
<v Debra Kaysen>And then you can check my levels.
<v Debra Kaysen>然后你可以检查我的电平。

00:43:00.310 --> 00:43:00.490
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:43:00.490 --> 00:43:01.560
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。

00:43:01.560 --> 00:43:10.340
<v Jaime Lowe>It's session eight, and Dr. Kaysen and I are making levels jokes. I'll check the level of her volume, and she'll check the levels of my PTSD symptoms.
<v Jaime Lowe>这是第八次疗程了，凯森博士和我正在开“电平”玩笑。我检查她的音量电平，她检查我的PTSD症状电平。

00:43:10.340 --> 00:43:19.110
<v Debra Kaysen>So since yesterday, how much have you been distressed by repeated disturbing and unwanted memories of the traumatic event?
<v Debra Kaysen>所以从昨天以来，你因反复出现创伤事件令人不安和不想要的记忆而感到痛苦的程度如何？

00:43:19.110 --> 00:43:20.460
<v Jaime Lowe>Two-- eh, one.
<v Jaime Lowe>二——呃，一。

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:21.020
<v Debra Kaysen>OK.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。

00:43:21.020 --> 00:43:22.210
<v Jaime Lowe>Probably one.
<v Jaime Lowe>可能是一。

00:43:22.210 --> 00:43:30.560
<v Ira Glass>Dr. Kaysen and I are starting to get into a nice, comfortable rhythm. And of course, there's only three sessions left, including this one.
<v Ira Glass>凯森博士和我开始进入一种舒适的节奏。当然，包括这次在内，只剩下三次疗程了。

00:43:30.560 --> 00:43:44.870
<v Ira Glass>It's Halloween, and she's got on themed earrings and spider web tights. In another setting, I might find this kind of ridiculous, but I know her enough to know it's genuine and wonderful. And I feel sad I'm not dressed up, too.
<v Ira Glass>今天是万圣节，她戴着主题耳环，穿着蜘蛛网连裤袜。在另一个场合，我可能会觉得这很荒谬，但我足够了解她，知道这是真诚且美妙的。我觉得我也没打扮感到难过。

00:43:44.870 --> 00:44:05.850
<v Ira Glass>We go through my big mama worksheets. Just as Dr. Kaysen said we would, we've been using the big worksheets to explore big themes. We've gone through safety and esteem. Today is control. And one of my stuck points is, when I'm not in control, bad things will happen, which I believe 80%.
<v Ira Glass>我们过了一遍“大妈”工作表。就像凯森博士所说的，我们一直在用大工作表探索大主题。我们已经走过了安全和自尊。今天是控制。我的卡住点之一是“当我失去控制时，坏事就会发生”，我对此有80%的相信。

00:44:05.850 --> 00:44:12.470
<v Debra Kaysen>So what evidence do you have that, if you are not in control, bad things will happen?
<v Debra Kaysen>那么你有什么证据表明，如果你没有控制，坏事就会发生？

00:44:12.470 --> 00:44:15.620
<v Jaime Lowe>Bad things have happened when I haven't been in control.
<v Jaime Lowe>坏事发生时我都没有控制。

00:44:15.620 --> 00:44:20.670
<v Debra Kaysen>OK. So you could say things in the past.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。所以你可以说过去发生的事件。

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:21.360
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:30.790
<v Debra Kaysen>OK? So I'm assuming like the sexual assault, events that happen when you are manic. OK. OK.
<v Debra Kaysen>好吗？所以我假设是像那次性侵，在你处于躁狂状态时发生的事件。好的。好的。

00:44:30.790 --> 00:44:56.980
<v Jaime Lowe>When Dr. Kaysen asks about mania, I pause, just sit there for a while. Then I nod without saying anything. Control is a big thing for me. It's a big thing in mania. You're not in control, but you think you are. Dr. Kaysen and I keep going through this sheet. My statement, when I'm not in control, bad things will happen-- is there evidence it might not be true?
<v Jaime Lowe>当凯森博士问到躁狂症时，我停顿了一下，在那儿坐了很久。然后我没说话，点了点头。控制对我来说是个大问题。在躁狂症中这更是一个大问题。你没有控制，但你认为你有。凯森博士和我继续看那张表。我的陈述，“当我失去控制时，坏事就会发生”——有证据表明这可能不是真的吗？

00:44:56.980 --> 00:44:59.460
<v Debra Kaysen>So bad things can happen when--
<v Debra Kaysen>所以坏事发生时——

00:44:59.460 --> 00:45:00.720
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm in control.
<v Jaime Lowe>我有控制。

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:07.340
<v Debra Kaysen>When you are in control-- yeah, absolutely. And have you ever been out of control and not had something bad happen?
<v Debra Kaysen>当你确实有控制时——对，绝对。而且你有没有过失去控制却没发生坏事的时候？

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:08.830
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>有。

00:45:08.830 --> 00:45:27.700
<v Ira Glass>We go over my answers to each of the little boxes on the worksheet. By the end, I have a new thought. Giving up control does not always mean bad things will happen. Dr. Kaysen asks, what feelings come up when I'm thinking about giving up control? Fear is the first one. And then--
<v Ira Glass>我们过了一遍我对工作表中每个小格子的回答。到最后，我有了个新想法。“放弃控制并不总是意味着坏事会发生。”凯森博士问，当我想到放弃控制时，产生了什么感觉？恐惧是第一个。然后——

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:29.780
<v Jaime Lowe>There's definitely a lot of guilt and shame.
<v Jaime Lowe>确实有很多罪恶感和羞耻感。

00:45:29.780 --> 00:45:30.280
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, so I--
<v Debra Kaysen>是的，所以我觉得——

00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:32.410
<v Jaime Lowe>I've really come around to the shame concept.
<v Jaime Lowe>我真的开始接受“羞耻”这个概念了。

00:45:32.410 --> 00:45:33.680
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, you've really been--
<v Debra Kaysen>是的，你确实一直在——

00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:35.930
<v Jaime Lowe>I'm really embracing it.
<v Jaime Lowe>我真的在拥抱它。

00:45:35.930 --> 00:45:39.110
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, you have. That's true. That is true.
<v Debra Kaysen>是的，你做到了。那确实是真的。那确实是真的。

00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:57.050
<v Jaime Lowe>We're joking, but there's something there. I'm realizing that, for me, shame is related to mental illness. After mania, it's hard not to want to be buried for a decade, until everyone forgets that you tried to start a hippie cult in a tutu covered in glitter and war paint.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们在开玩笑，但这里面确实有东西。我意识到，对我来说，羞耻感与精神疾病有关。在躁狂发作后，很难不想要隐姓埋名十年，直到每个人都忘记了你试图在一个穿着带亮片的芭蕾舞裙、涂着战彩的嬉皮教派中开场。

00:45:57.050 --> 00:46:00.600
<v Jaime Lowe>Today, at the end of the session, my homework is a little different.
<v Jaime Lowe>今天，疗程结束时，我的作业有点不同。

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:06.360
<v Debra Kaysen>And then I want you to practice giving one compliment and receiving one compliment.
<v Debra Kaysen>然后我想要你练习给别人一个恭维，并接受一个恭维。

00:46:06.360 --> 00:46:08.190
<v Jaime Lowe>I don't really speak to people that much.
<v Jaime Lowe>我不怎么和别人说话。

00:46:08.190 --> 00:46:12.080
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, now I know. So it would mean you'd have to speak to people.
<v Debra Kaysen>是的，现在我知道了。所以这意味着你必须和人说话。

00:46:12.080 --> 00:46:13.750
<v Jaime Lowe>Give one compliment, and--
<v Jaime Lowe>给一个恭维，和——

00:46:13.750 --> 00:46:15.010
<v Debra Kaysen>And receive one compliment.
<v Debra Kaysen>接受一个恭维。

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:17.720
<v Jaime Lowe>How do I make someone compliment me?
<v Jaime Lowe>我怎么让别人恭维我？

00:46:17.720 --> 00:46:20.810
<v Debra Kaysen>So that's tough, because you're in a strange city.
<v Debra Kaysen>所以这很难，因为你在一个陌生的城市。

00:46:20.810 --> 00:46:23.210
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:46:23.210 --> 00:46:30.400
<v Debra Kaysen>So what it's going to mean is a couple of things. Either you're going to need to listen for compliments you're getting naturally without filtering them.
<v Debra Kaysen>所以这意味着几件事。要么你需要倾听你自然得到的恭维而不过滤它们。

00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:31.310
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:46:31.310 --> 00:46:37.820
<v Debra Kaysen>Or sometimes if you're interacting with people, then you may be more likely to get compliments.
<v Debra Kaysen>或者有时如果你和别人互动，那么你更有可能得到恭维。

00:46:37.820 --> 00:46:57.380
<v Jaime Lowe>This is the point of the exercise. Dr. Kaysen is preparing me for life post-treatment by encouraging me to interact with people. She explains that social isolation often follows PTSD.
<v Jaime Lowe>这就是练习的目的。凯森博士鼓励我与人互动，为治疗后的生活做准备。她解释说PTSD之后经常会伴随社会孤立。

00:46:57.380 --> 00:46:59.070
<v Jaime Lowe>Session nine.
<v Jaime Lowe>第九次疗程。

00:46:59.070 --> 00:47:01.390
<v Debra Kaysen>All right. Well, let's dig in to how the practice went.
<v Debra Kaysen>好的。那么，让我们来探讨一下练习进展如何。

00:47:01.390 --> 00:47:02.300
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:08.840
<v Debra Kaysen>Before we go into worksheets, how did the giving and receiving compliments go?
<v Debra Kaysen>在进入工作表之前，给别人恭维和接受恭维怎么样了？

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:22.010
<v Jaime Lowe>Yesterday when Dr. Kaysen gave me the compliment assignment, it seemed difficult and silly. It felt like I entered Mr. Roger's land. And even though I know he was a good soul, I was always a Muppets Show person.
<v Jaime Lowe>昨天凯森博士给我布置恭维作业时，它看起来很难也很傻。感觉像我进入了罗杰斯先生的邻里。尽管我知道他是个好人，但我一直是芝麻街木偶秀的粉丝。

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:36.170
<v Jaime Lowe>I wasn't entirely sure how to conjure up compliments. As I anticipated, giving compliments was pretty easy. I told Dr. Kaysen that I told my Airbnb guy I liked his record collection and his illustrations.
<v Jaime Lowe>我不完全确定怎么变出恭维。正如我预料的，给别人恭维相当容易。我告诉凯森博士，我告诉我的爱彼迎房东我喜欢他的唱片收藏和插画。

00:47:36.170 --> 00:47:39.620
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah, nice. Did you get any compliments?
<v Debra Kaysen>是的，好。你得到恭维了吗？

00:47:39.620 --> 00:47:42.020
<v Jaime Lowe>I did. It was really funny, actually.
<v Jaime Lowe>得到了。其实真的很有趣。

00:47:42.020 --> 00:47:42.840
<v Debra Kaysen>[LAUGHS]
<v Debra Kaysen>[笑]

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:48.530
<v Jaime Lowe>I got a very-- a really, really, really big compliment, to me.
<v Jaime Lowe>我得到了一个——对我来说真的、真的、真的很大的恭维。

00:47:48.530 --> 00:47:53.920
<v Debra Kaysen>Yeah.
<v Debra Kaysen>是的。

00:47:53.920 --> 00:47:57.370
<v Jaime Lowe>I pause. I'm choking up.
<v Jaime Lowe>我停顿了一下。我哽咽了。

00:47:57.370 --> 00:48:09.190
<v Jaime Lowe>[SNIFFS] Someone wrote me an email [SNIFFS] just about my book.
<v Jaime Lowe>[抽泣] 有人给我写了一封邮件[抽泣]，关于我的书。

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:18.820
<v Ira Glass>About my book, Mental, the memoir I wrote about being bipolar. The email was from a young woman who is also bipolar.
<v Ira Glass>关于我的书，《Mental》，那本我写的关于躁郁症的回忆录。邮件来自一个同样患有躁郁症的年轻女性。

00:48:18.820 --> 00:48:33.660
<v Jaime Lowe>She had to leave college, and go home, and kind of really shift her plans. And she said that she really felt like the story that I told was something that she had never read, but could really relate to. It was a really big compliment.
<v Jaime Lowe>她不得不休学，回家，真的大幅度调整了她的计划。她说她真的觉得我讲的故事是她从未读过，但真的能产生共鸣的。那是一个非常大的恭维。

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:36.460
<v Debra Kaysen>That's powerful.
<v Debra Kaysen>这很有力量。

00:48:36.460 --> 00:48:46.940
<v Jaime Lowe>We'd been talking in a previous session about my feelings of being a failure, of not doing well at my work. Dr. Kaysen wants me to remember this email.
<v Jaime Lowe>我们在之前的疗程中讨论过我关于“失败者”的感觉，关于在工作中做得不好。凯森博士希望我记住这封邮件。

00:48:46.940 --> 00:48:57.580
<v Jaime Lowe>I can try and remember it. [LAUGHS] It's hard. It's really hard for me to remember the positive responses, like, to anything.
<v Jaime Lowe>我可以试着记住它。[笑] 这很难。对我来说，记住积极的回应真的很难，就像，任何东西的积极回应。

00:48:57.580 --> 00:49:03.390
<v Debra Kaysen>Yep. And so this is one for you to practice, right? So we're starting to move into life skills, right?
<v Debra Kaysen>是的。所以这是你需要练习的一个，对吧？所以我们开始进入生活技能了，对吧？

00:49:03.390 --> 00:49:03.760
<v Jaime Lowe>Yeah.
<v Jaime Lowe>是的。

00:49:03.760 --> 00:49:21.280
<v Debra Kaysen>This is one that's going to be a life skill for you. What am I leaving out? That's been a really helpful question for you. Right? It forces you to get out of that negative space and look for things that don't fit with the stuck point.
<v Debra Kaysen>这将是一个给你的生活技能。“我遗漏了什么？”这对你来说一直是一个非常有帮助的问题。对吧？它强迫你跳出那个消极的空间，去寻找那些不符合卡住点的事物。

00:49:21.280 --> 00:49:34.510
<v Jaime Lowe>This is the next to last session, and I can feel we're coming to the end. The way Dr. Kaysen is talking and the way I'm feeling, it just feels close to over. And then my last homework assignment is revealed.
<v Jaime Lowe>这是倒数第二次疗程了，我能感觉到我们快要结束了。凯森博士说话的方式和我感觉的方式，感觉快要结束了。然后我的最后家庭作业揭晓了。

00:49:34.510 --> 00:49:41.050
<v Debra Kaysen>I want you to write at least one page on what you think, now, about why the traumatic event occurred.
<v Debra Kaysen>我想要你写至少一页关于你现在怎么看待为什么那个创伤事件发生了。

00:49:41.050 --> 00:49:41.770
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:49:41.770 --> 00:50:01.510
<v Ira Glass>It makes sense. If one purpose of this therapy is to change the story you're telling yourself, it would be a good gauge to write a before and after, to see how the narrative shifts-- the perfect book ends.
<v Ira Glass>这说得通。如果这种疗法的目的之一是改变你告诉自己的故事，那么写一份“之前”和“之后”来查看叙事是如何转变的将是一个很好的衡量标准——完美的结尾。

00:50:01.510 --> 00:50:04.000
<v Ira Glass>Session ten.
<v Ira Glass>第十次疗程。

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:06.490
<v Debra Kaysen>How was it writing the second impact statement?
<v Debra Kaysen>写第二份“影响陈述”感觉如何？

00:50:06.490 --> 00:50:10.930
<v Jaime Lowe>It was good. It felt like it was less fraught.
<v Jaime Lowe>挺好的。感觉少了很多焦虑。

00:50:10.930 --> 00:50:57.420
<v Ira Glass>It's the day of my last session, and it's like Seattle knows it. This morning, I rode the ferry and saw a double rainbow. I walked around Bainbridge Island with a friend and saw sun, real sun through trees and abandoned mills, sun shining against the sound, and against graffiti, and against soaring seagulls, sun that reflected on the sign for Bernie's Automotive Service that read, "Welcome to the team, Charlie." It all looked shining and miraculous. I could feel the excitement of being done. And the Pacific Northwest felt it with me.
<v Ira Glass>这是我最后一次疗程的那天，就像西雅图知道一样。今天早上，我乘轮渡看到了双彩虹。我和一个朋友在贝恩布里奇岛散步，看到了太阳，真正的阳光穿过树林和废弃的磨坊，阳光照在海峡上，照在涂鸦上，照在翱翔的海鸥上，阳光反射在伯尼汽车服务公司的招牌上，写着“欢迎加入团队，查理”。这一切看起来光芒万丈、奇迹般。我能感受到结束的兴奋。太平洋西北地区也和我一起感受到了。

00:50:57.420 --> 00:51:05.970
<v Ira Glass>Writing the second impact statement was easier than writing the first one. I didn't feel pent up emotions spilling over. I didn't feel out of control.
<v Ira Glass>写第二份影响陈述比第一份容易得多。我没感到压抑的情绪溢出。我没感到失去控制。

00:51:05.970 --> 00:51:09.080
<v Debra Kaysen>Nice. So I'm going to have you read it to me.
<v Debra Kaysen>好。我要你读给我听。

00:51:09.080 --> 00:51:09.400
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:51:09.400 --> 00:51:13.710
<v Debra Kaysen>OK?
<v Debra Kaysen>好吗？

00:51:13.710 --> 00:51:40.150
<v Jaime Lowe>The attack happened because-- who knows why it happened? I don't know why he molested me. I don't know what motivated him. I know that it didn't happen because I was there, or wearing boxers, or friendly, or willing it upon myself. I was young and walking to school, not looking for a sexual assault. That's pretty improbable. Why would anyone look for that?
<v Jaime Lowe>那次袭击发生是因为——谁知道为什么发生？我不知道他为什么猥亵我。我不知道是什么动机驱使他。我知道那不是因为我在那儿，或穿着平角裤，或表现友好，或故意招惹。我那时很小，走在去学校的路上，没在寻找性侵。那太不可能了。谁会寻找那种事？

00:51:40.150 --> 00:52:03.430
<v Jaime Lowe>I think it happened because-- I just really can't explain why. He was in control. He had a knife. I did everything I could to protect myself and to get away. My feelings after it happened were natural. My response was as good as it could be. Good is the wrong word, but I think I did OK, considering all of the circumstances.
<v Jaime Lowe>我想它发生是因为——我实在无法解释为什么。他控制了局面。他有把刀。我尽我所能保护自己并逃脱。我发生后的感觉是自然的。我的反应尽可能好了。好这个词用得不对，但我想我做得还可以，考虑到所有的环境。

00:52:03.430 --> 00:52:11.870
<v Ira Glass>I keep going. And when I get to the end, Dr. Kaysen reads back what I wrote after our first session, my original impact statement.
<v Ira Glass>我继续读。当我读完时，凯森博士读回了我们在第一次疗程后写的东西，我最初的影响陈述。

00:52:11.870 --> 00:52:14.040
<v Debra Kaysen>So let me read back to you what you wrote the first time.
<v Debra Kaysen>让我读回你第一次写的东西。

00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:18.500
<v Jaime Lowe>OK.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:52:18.500 --> 00:52:34.620
<v Debra Kaysen>I was young, 13, and I don't think I knew much about actual human nature-- friendliness, strangers. It's funny to think that I was not cautious about the very thing kids are always warned of-- strangers. I walked to my bus stop alone every morning.
<v Debra Kaysen>“我当时很小，13岁，我觉得我不了解人性——友善，陌生人。有趣的是，我竟然没对孩子们常被叮嘱要防范的东西——陌生人——保持警惕。我每天早上独自走去巴士站。”

00:52:34.620 --> 00:52:39.180
<v Jaime Lowe>Dr. Kaysen keeps reading, and I can hear how my story has changed.
<v Jaime Lowe>凯森博士继续读，我能听出我的故事发生了怎样的变化。

00:52:39.180 --> 00:52:41.820
<v Debra Kaysen>So what changes do you hear?
<v Debra Kaysen>你听到了什么变化？

00:52:41.820 --> 00:52:49.130
<v Jaime Lowe>Well, I think there's a huge shift of focus. Now, it feels like there's just a different way of seeing it.
<v Jaime Lowe>嗯，我想关注点发生了巨大的转变。现在，感觉只是有一种不同的看待方式。

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:50.160
<v Debra Kaysen>Right.
<v Debra Kaysen>对。

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:55.390
<v Jaime Lowe>Like, that attack just doesn't feel as potent, I think.
<v Jaime Lowe>就像，那次袭击感觉没那么强大了，我想。

00:52:55.390 --> 00:53:07.410
<v Debra Kaysen>Yep. Yep. That's what I heard, too. And also, really an acceptance that you may never know-- in fact, you probably will never know-- exactly why it happened. But you can be really confident that it didn't have anything to do with you.
<v Debra Kaysen>对。对。我也听到了。而且，真的接受了你可能永远不会知道——事实上，你可能永远不会知道——它到底为什么发生。但你可以非常自信它与你无关。

00:53:07.410 --> 00:53:15.720
<v Jaime Lowe>Right. Which is something that I do think I intellectually understood, but it wasn't something I accepted, I don't think.
<v Jaime Lowe>对。这是我理性上确实理解的东西，但我觉得这不是我接受的东西。

00:53:15.720 --> 00:53:16.230
<v Debra Kaysen>Right.
<v Debra Kaysen>对。

00:53:16.230 --> 00:53:23.400
<v Jaime Lowe>And I think that in the difference between the first statement to the last, there was acceptance in that.
<v Jaime Lowe>而且我认为在第一份陈述到最后一份陈述之间，有一种接受感。

00:53:23.400 --> 00:53:51.660
<v Ira Glass>Acceptance that it wasn't me, that it wasn't my fault. Dr. Kaysen hands me a print-out of the PTSD check-ins that we did at the beginning of each session. By the end, my number has dropped from the initial total by about 12 points. She says 10 points indicates meaningful change. The number doesn't mean that much to me, but I do feel better. I know that. She wonders how I feel about the therapy, overall.
<v Ira Glass>接受它不是我，不是我的错。凯森博士递给我一份我们在每次疗程开始时做的PTSD检查清单的打印件。到最后，我的分数比最初的总分下降了约12点。她说10点表示有意义的转变。这个数字对我来说意义不大，但我确实感觉好多了。我知道。她想知道我总体上对这种治疗感觉如何。

00:53:51.660 --> 00:53:58.180
<v Debra Kaysen>What did you notice in the process? What was helpful for you? What was not helpful? How have you seen shifts?
<v Debra Kaysen>你在过程中注意到了什么？什么对你有帮助？什么没有帮助？你是怎么看到转变的？

00:53:58.180 --> 00:54:27.840
<v Jaime Lowe>Well, I've definitely seen-- I mean, I think working through all of this stuff about the assault was incredibly helpful, because I felt like there was a lot of unresolved assumptions that I didn't really even recognize were there. And that it was, frankly, kind of a relief to not really have that there as much. Like it just kind of feels better.
<v Jaime Lowe>嗯，我确实看到了——我的意思是，我觉得处理所有这些关于侵犯的事情非常有帮助，因为我觉得有很多我甚至都没意识到存在的未解决的假设。说实话，没有那么多那种假设作为负担，这真是一种解脱。就像，感觉好多了。

00:54:27.840 --> 00:55:00.670
<v Ira Glass>The process of CPT surprised me. Its effectiveness surprised me. I could see in just those two statements how the ten sessions in between shifted my thinking. This is like ninja therapy. They distract you with the weird language, and then progress creeps up on you from behind. As always, Dr. Kaysen is huggable, gracious, warm. I'm going to miss her. I'm sad to leave, even though I know it's time, and I know I can.
<v Ira Glass>CPT的过程让我感到惊讶。它的有效性让我感到惊讶。我从那两份陈述中看到了中间十次疗程是如何转变我的想法的。这就像忍者治疗。他们用古怪的语言分散你的注意力，然后进步从后面悄悄地靠近你。一如既往，凯森博士是那样可拥抱、优雅、温暖。我会想念她的。我很难过要离开，尽管我知道是时候了，我知道我可以。

00:55:00.670 --> 00:55:01.500
<v Jaime Lowe>Is that it?
<v Jaime Lowe>就这些吗？

00:55:01.500 --> 00:55:02.070
<v Debra Kaysen>That's it.
<v Debra Kaysen>就这些。

00:55:02.070 --> 00:55:03.130
<v Jaime Lowe>That's the end?
<v Jaime Lowe>这就结束了？

00:55:03.130 --> 00:55:04.790
<v Debra Kaysen>The end.
<v Debra Kaysen>结束。

00:55:04.790 --> 00:55:14.400
<v Jaime Lowe>All right.
<v Jaime Lowe>好的。

00:55:14.400 --> 00:55:34.880
<v Ira Glass>It's been 10 months since I did CPT. I still have my folder stuffed with more than 100 worksheets. The spine is reinforced with neon orange duct tape, because the folder is falling apart, but I'm not. I feel like I did, in fact, get something I wanted out of this, more than what I expected.
<v Ira Glass>自从我做CPT已经过去10个月了。我的文件夹里依然塞满了100多份工作表。书脊用霓虹橙色胶带加固过，因为文件夹快散架了，但我没有。我觉得我确实从这儿得到了我想要的，比预期的还要多。

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:59.410
<v Ira Glass>I don't feel as hopeless and incapacitated when I hear about other assaults. The news doesn't dictate my emotional state in the same way. When Jeffrey Epstein was found dead, I was angry on behalf of his victims. I shouted a few expletives and threw my phone on the ground, but I could still function. And really, that's a very rational reaction.
<v Ira Glass>当我听到其他侵犯事件时，我没感到那么绝望和无能为力。新闻不会以同样的方式支配我的情绪状态。当杰弗里·爱泼斯坦被发现死亡时，我为他的受害者感到愤怒。我喊了几句脏话并把手机扔在地上，但我依然能正常工作。真的，那是一个非常理性的反应。

00:55:59.410 --> 00:56:26.720
<v Ira Glass>Before CPT, I had a lot of anxiety and overwhelming feelings. I was experiencing PTSD symptoms, which I didn't even realize. I had my suspicions that these intense emotions were related to the assault, but it was never named for me. Now, my symptoms are mostly gone. And when problems come up, I can work them through in my head, or I pull out a blank worksheet.
<v Ira Glass>CPT之前，我有很多焦虑和难以承受的情绪。我经历了PTSD症状，而我甚至没意识到。我怀疑这些强烈的情绪与侵犯有关，但从未有人为我命名。现在，我的症状基本消失了。当问题出现时，我可以在脑子里梳理它们，或者我拿出一张空白工作表。

00:56:26.720 --> 00:56:59.230
<v Ira Glass>There's a surge of women seeking help right now. A National Sexual Assault hotline saw a 200% increase over normal volume after the Kavanaugh hearing. There was a 20% increase in calls after the R. Kelly doc aired. After the sentencing hearing of Larry Nassar, calls to the same hotline increased by 46%. Of course, so many survivors of sexual assault don't get any treatment at all-- not talk therapy, not CPT, nothing.
<v Ira Glass>现在有大量女性寻求帮助。卡瓦诺听证会后，全美性侵热线的呼叫量比正常水平增加了200%。R·凯利纪录片播出后，呼叫量增加了20%。拉里·纳萨尔量刑听证会后，打给同一条热线的呼叫增加了46%。当然，太多的性侵幸存者根本得不到任何治疗——没有谈话疗法，没有CPT，什么都没有。

00:56:59.230 --> 00:57:26.350
<v Ira Glass>When I started CPT, it was hard to say I'd been sexually assaulted out loud. I think I carried a lot of shame-- the word I was most offended by. But now I can say it, and I can say it without crying. I was sexually assaulted when I was 13.
<v Ira Glass>当我开始做CPT时，很难大声说出我遭到过性侵。我想我背负了太多羞耻感——那个我最反感的词。但现在我能说出来，而且说的时候不会流泪。我在13岁时遭到过性侵。

00:57:26.350 --> 00:57:36.120
<v Ira Glass>Jaime Lowe, she's the author of a memoir called Mental. In the month since she saw Dr. Debra Kaysen, Dr. Kaysen has moved from the University of Washington to Stanford.
<v Ira Glass>杰米·洛，她是回忆录《Mental》的作者。在见到黛布拉·凯森博士后的一个月里，凯森博士从华盛顿大学调到了斯坦福大学。

00:57:36.120 --> 00:58:04.120
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - MARY J. BLIGE, "THERAPY"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 玛丽·布莱姬，《Therapy》]

